 ## Background
-Recursive Ray Tracing was introduced by Turner Whitted in 1980 in his paper "An Improved Illumination Model for Shaded Display" [S1, unofficial]. It extended the basic ray casting algorithm by adding support for specular reflection and refraction, allowing for the rendering of mirrors and glass. The term "higher-order rays" refers to the secondary rays (reflection/refraction) spawned from primary rays [S1, unofficial].
+Recursive Ray Tracing was introduced as an extension to basic ray casting to handle diffuse reflection, specular reflection, and transmission by recursively tracing rays upon intersection with objects [S2, unofficial]. The term "higher-order rays" refers to the secondary rays spawned from primary rays [S1, unofficial]. It is used in volume rendering pipelines to evaluate local illumination, shadows, and ambient occlusion within participating media [S1, unofficial].
 
